<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Western Satraps</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Shaka satraps</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">Satraps</a> were a dynasty of Saka (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythian</a>) rulers who governed western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>, from around the first to the fourth century CE. They controlled important ports and trade routes. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a> discuss their conquest by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-ii">Chandragupta II</a>, which brought the wealthy western regions and their maritime trade under Gupta control.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Western Satraps, or Western Kshatrapas, were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythian</a> rulers of the western and central parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Saurashtra</a> in the south and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> in the east, covering modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>. They are called Western to distinguish them from the Northern Satraps, who ruled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> until the 2nd century CE. They were contemporaries of the Kushans in the north, to whom they were possibly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, and of the Satavahanas in Central India. The word kshatrapa has the same origin as satrap, from a Median word meaning viceroy or governor.</p><p>The line is thought to have begun with the short-lived <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshaharata-dynasty">Kshaharata dynasty</a>, whose earliest known member is Abhiraka. His successor Bhumaka was the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahapana">Nahapana</a>, who occupied parts of the Satavahana empire, held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and northern Konkan, and established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coinage of the Kshatrapas. His son-in-law Ushavadata governed the southern part of his territory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A new family, the Bhadramukhas or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kardamaka-dynasty">Kardamaka dynasty</a>, was founded by the satrap Castana, whose reign many place from 78 CE, which would make him the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saka-era">Saka era</a> used on his descendants' coins. Castana was satrap of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a>, and Ptolemy describes his territory as running from Patalene in the west to Ujjain in the east. A statue found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> beside those of the Kushan kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanishka">Kanishka</a> and Vima Taktu, bearing the name Shastana, suggests he may have been a Kushan feudatory.</p><p>Power began to decline in the 2nd century CE after the Saka rulers were defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautamiputra-satakarni">Gautamiputra Satakarni</a> of the Satavahanas. The kingdom revived, with a new family founded by Rudrasimha II, who ruled 304 to 348 CE. Under him Saka rule extended to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidisha">Vidisha</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanchi">Sanchi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eran">Eran</a> area, where an inscription records a well built by the Saka chief Sridharavarman. The Western Satraps were finally defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-ii">Chandragupta II</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a> in the 4th century CE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Their coinage was based on that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek</a> kings, with Greek or pseudo-Greek legends and royal busts on the obverse, and on the reverse a thunderbolt and an arrow, later a three-arched hill with a river, a crescent and the sun, in Brahmi. From the reigns of Jivadaman and Rudrasimha I the minting date is usually given in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saka-era">Saka era</a>, and each king's father is named, which has helped to clarify early Indian history. Buddhist monuments continued under them: the site of Devnimori in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> has viharas and a stupa in which coins of Rudrasimha were found, and its Buddha images show the influence of Gandhara. The Western Satraps may have helped transmit Gandhara's art to the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Western Satraps” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Chandragupta II (c. 375–415 CE) extended the empire westward by defeating the <b>Shaka satraps</b> of western India.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The main rivals, however, were the <b>Western Satraps</b>, Saka rulers of Gujarat, Malwa and western India who were probably at first subordinate to the Kushan Empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/satavahana-dynasty">Satavahana dynasty · Military</a></li></ul></section>
